 {:name "pytest", :kind "module", :text "Stdlib `pytest` subset: collection, assert introspection, conftest, parametrize, marks, `raises`/`warns`/`approx`, the common fixtures, and `pytest.main(args)` over node ids. Not supported: plugins, most CLI options, import-time assertion rewriting.\n\nClasses:\n  ExceptionInfo(tup) — The exception a `raises` block caught: `.type`, `.value`, `.traceback`, `.match(regex)`.\n  Failed(msg='') — The outcome `fail()` raises: the test was failed deliberately.\n  FixtureRequest(manager, nodeid, func=None, cls=None, module=None) — The `request` fixture: the running test's `nodeid`, `param`, `function`, plus `getfixtur…\n  LineMatcher(lines) — Matches an output listing against patterns -- `fnmatch_lines`, `re_match_lines`, `no_fnm…\n  MonkeyPatch() — The `monkeypatch` fixture: `setattr`, `delattr`, `setitem`, `setenv`, `chdir`, `syspath_…\n  OutcomeException(msg='') — Base class of the outcomes tests raise -- `Skipped`, `Failed`, `XFailed`; carries `.msg`.\n  Pytester(request) — A throwaway project directory for testing pytest itself: write files, then `runpytest()`.\n  RunResult(ret, out, err, rep, deselected=0) — The outcome of a `Pytester` run: `ret`, `stdout`/`stderr` line matchers and `assert_outc…\n  Skipped(msg='') — The outcome `skip()` raises: the test asked not to run.\n  UsageError(...) — Raised when pytest itself was called wrongly -- a bad argument, not a failing test.\n  XFailed(msg='') — The outcome `xfail()` raises: this test was expected to fail.\n  approx(expected, rel=None, abs=None, nan_ok=False) — Compares numbers loosely: `assert 0.1 + 0.2 == approx(0.3)`, with `rel`/`abs`/`nan_ok`.\n\nFunctions:\n  console_main(args=None, ns=None) — Runs the tests in a namespace and prints the terminal report; answers pytest's exit code.\n  exit(reason='', returncode=None) — Ends the whole run immediately with that reason and exit code.\n  fail(reason='', pytrace=True) — Fails the running test with that reason (raises, so nothing after it runs).\n  fixture(func=None, scope='function', params=None, autouse=False, name=None, ids=None) — Marks a function as a fixture -- `@pytest.fixture`, or `@pytest.fixture(scope=\"module\", …\n  importorskip(modname, minversion=None, reason=None) — Imports a module or skips the test when it is missing -- `pytest.importorskip(\"numpy\")`.\n  main(args=None, ns=None) — Runs the tests in a namespace and prints the terminal report; answers pytest's exit code.\n  param(*values, **kwargs) — One parametrised case with its own marks and id: `pytest.param(2, id=\"two\")`.\n  raises(expected_exception, *args, **kwargs) — Asserts an exception is raised: a context manager (`with pytest.raises(ValueError, match…\n  skip(reason='', allow_module_level=False) — Skips the running test with that reason (raises, so nothing after it runs).\n  warns(expected_warning=<class 'Warning'>, *args, **kwargs) — Asserts a warning is raised: a context manager, or `warns(UserWarning, fn, *args)`.\n  xfail(reason='') — Marks the running test as an expected failure and stops it.\n\nData:\n  mark\n\nRead one member: doc(\"pytest.ExceptionInfo\")"}
